{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head 6daab7c36885b1b6129a37164dae8aec196f01a4. Each of your six points holds. No real host token and no Worker ran this, as your note states.\n\n**1. No definition: right.** The refusal belongs in custody. It reaches every room on deployment, and a pin would reach only new rooms. A refused act writes no entry, so a definition would give replay nothing to read at that act, and the credential route writes no entry at all. Replay already takes the membership observation as given, so this adds one more input of that kind. The docs state the cost.\n\n**2. The transaction is one, and I read this and did not run a failure.** `Turns.#commit` calls `#judged` inside `store.transaction` (`turn.ts:322`), and `verdict.sealed` runs there (`turn.ts:361`). The `CredentialStore` uses the same object's SQL (`object.ts:211`), and `transactionSync` covers it. `sealedDelivery` is called directly, outside the `try` that swallows a port's failure in `core.ts:260-266`. A throw is not `Full`, `Unfit`, `StandingError` or `AmountError`, so `#commit` rethrows it, the transaction rolls back, and `deliver` writes no entry. The note's sentence \"A failure in `sealedDelivery` aborts the delivery\" has no case or control behind it. That is acceptable, because the only throws are a storage failure and a missing scope. A later edit can mark it as read.\n\nNo other path applies the notice. `delivery.ts:296` is the only `sealed` that writes a delivery's request entry, and the other writers at `core.ts` and `operations.ts` write acts and outcomes. The `compromised` handler declares no `order`, so `superseded` at `derive/src/delivery.ts:203` does not reach it. `compromisedKey` destructures the body only after `decision === \"applied\"` on a `tell`, and a handler matched that body by its `message`, so it cannot throw on a missing body.\n\n**3. The bound holds, and more firmly than the note says.** `floorEnabled` reads only `wiring.fixtureArchive` (`object.ts:296`), so no deployed wiring takes a floor at any scope today, and `archive-target.ts:89` excludes membership as an archive target. \"Membership journals take no floor\" in `docs/hosts.md` is true of this code. A future reviewed floor policy for a membership pin would break it, and `archive-inventory.ts:27` already states that exclusion. The rows also stay within what a room's admins can cause, because the guard accepts only this room's directory, and the directory's guard accepts only its membership.\n\n**4. Keep `evidence-recover-timeout`.** It is a `transition` on `branch` at `destination-evidence-v17.ts:476`. It opens no `mint-read` and yields no credential. `mint-read` opens only from `read-token` (`destination.ts:525-527`), and the credential route demands `opening.signed.intent.kind === \"read-token\"` (`destination-host.ts:378`). So reading the kind closes the token route, and a check on the grant would refuse an act that gives no read. The key's other acts remain the wider question, and the docs list it as open.\n\n**5. `compromisedKey` reads the same field on every pin.** I ran a scratch script over the catalog, read-only, from the worktree: destination @1 to @17, directory @1 to @23, and membership @1 to @10. Each destination declares the `compromised` receive with `fields.key`. Each directory forwards `{ field: \"key\" }`, each membership sends `{ slot: \"id\" }`, and destination @2 to @17 all hold `read-token`. `directory-partition@1` has no forward, and membership tells the root directory. @16's extra guard is `has-repository`, which also guards every act there, so a room with no repository has no token to refuse. This reads the declarations. It does not run older pins.\n\n**6. Rita's step: correct.** After the notice, the key takes no new token. Before it, the key can mint for up to 300 seconds and read for up to 600. \"Step 4, or 600 seconds after step 3\" covers that with no measured delay, and I agree with keeping the wait. The new line \"Use `as=compromised` in step 3\" is right, because `as=retired` sends no notice at `membership.ts:523`. An operator can see the notice with `dap log` on the destination, and a later edit may add that, but it does not change the safe order.\n\n**Also traced.** `worker.ts:703` spreads `given` into `repositoryAuthority`, so `named` reaches `observing` in the one production wiring. The commit's check runs first in `held` (`authority.ts:274`), so an active observation does not win. A named key with no observation gets `authority-unavailable`, which gives no token. The credential check comes first at `destination-host.ts:371`, and the MCP tools use the same route. A repeat of an earlier `read-token` gives a receipt and no way to take the token.\n\n**Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` is a0cda48b…, every digest verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's `head.txt` is fdfc1564, exit 0, with 3476 and 976 tests. No path outside docs and notes changes from abbe2646 to fdfc1564, and the head adds only the note and its manifest. The earlier gate's timeout at `application-examples.test.ts:63` is kept and labelled. I ran `token-refusal.test.ts` and `authority.test.ts` (6 passed), `pin-native-declarations --check`, `security-entry-points` (10 pass), check-notes (0 warnings), check-doc-links (800, 0 broken) and check-commit-messages (4, 0 flagged). The worktree stays clean. I did not rerun the controls.\n\n**Architecture.** One narrow port, `sealedDelivery`, and one private table. The fold and the definitions do not change. The rollback claim holds: older code ignores the table, and no Durable Object migration runs.\n\n**Security.** This closes the two routes that I raised in 02639e3e once the notice arrives. It does not end a token already read, and the time before the notice stays open, as the docs say. A stranger cannot close a key, because only an applied notice writes a row. The refusal is permanent, which fits a compromised key that can never return.\n\n**Simplification.** Nothing to remove. A lookup by primary key beats a scan of the journal, and a floor could remove the entry that such a scan needs.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6c9db26123548120e71ff2979fb215dcbc9db922","binding":"assigned","head":"6daab7c36885b1b6129a37164dae8aec196f01a4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:70cccd9d065ca265bfed76638c8a93eff06c9d44\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c004795751b14d4f198644fec200106ab7198440\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3143e4f1b266a018a92520e74c2a46c05ddfdbbd","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}